Summary:

Some people said they experienced similar problems.
Some suggested that the CPU might be bad. I had DEC
replace this, but it made no difference. Eventually,
DEC said it was bad memory.  HSR Sys-Admin pegged it
with this response:

> in 80-90% of the cases memory-boards are bad!

Well, there you have it.  My second question was
should I care about them.  The majority said that I
should, especially if there is more than one. Again,
HSR Sys-Admin pointed out that the error rate can get
very high:

> yes, as in that case the problems will later on come in the rate
> of 1 message/millisecond!

alan had useful info about uerf to get more info:

> Try the "-o full" option on uerf(8) or dia(8) on V4.0

DEC field service had me use this command to generate
a hex dump from which they were able to determine that
bank 0 on my system is flaky:

uerf -R -r 100 -Z

Both the POST and the console test revealed no errors with the
memory. memx, however, did tweak the flaky address(es) and
cause errors to show up in the log.

